  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, temperatures across the area are in the mid 70s with \nwinds out of the south. There are a couple small cells still making \ntheir way northwest to southeast across central and northeast SD, \nthey are not expected to be severe. Highs today will be a little \ncooler, generally in the low to mid 90s with little difference in \nheat index values. Also today, a plume of smoke is expected to move \nin both aloft and at the surface. This will cause some hazy skies \nand slight reductions in visibility at the surface. Winds across \nnorthern SD today are expected to gust between 30 and 40 mph out of \nthe north to northwest. These winds, combined with dry fuels and \nrelative humidity values across north central SD dropping to around \n20% this afternoon will drive enhanced grassland fire danger \nconcerns. Therefore, a Red Flag Warning is in effect for portions of \nnorth central SD from 2 PM to 8 PM MDT.\n\nTemperatures for the rest of the week will be rather significantly \ncooler, with highs in the 80s through Thursday for areas west river \nand through Friday for areas east of the Missouri River. This coming \nweekend again looks to be significantly above normal with highs in \nthe mid 90s to low 100s. Humidity at this time does not look to be \nsignificant enough to raise heat index values to advisory criteria, \nbut this could change as we get closer. High pressure moves in \nTuesday night, moving southeast across the area Wednesday. Behind \nthe high, a low moves into eastern Colorado bringing a chance of \nthunderstorms to the western forecast area Thursday. Its hard to say \nif any storms will be severe at this time. As mentioned, heat \nreturns Friday through the weekend and this looks to cut off our \nchances for storms for the most part.",
